WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.386
Bill C-63, the Online Harms Act.

00:00:03.386 --> 00:00:26.318
This analysis examines who lobbied for online content regulation, the powers of the new Digital Safety Commission including penalties up to twenty-five million dollars or five percent of global revenue, mandatory twenty-four hour content removal timelines, pre-crime peace bond provisions that allow courts to restrain expression before an offence occurs, and increased penalties including life imprisonment for advocating genocide.

00:00:26.318 --> 00:00:30.920
All mapped against Charter Section 2(b) free expression protections.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.340
What Bill C-63 does.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:42.215
The Online Harms Act creates a Digital Safety Commission with enforcement powers, a Digital Safety Ombudsperson, and mandatory content removal timelines.

00:00:42.215 --> 00:00:45.499
It amends the Criminal Code and Canadian Human Rights Act.

00:00:45.499 --> 00:00:56.761
Key provisions: twenty-four hour removal for flagged content, peace bonds before offences occur inverting the burden of proof, and life imprisonment for advocating genocide — up from five years.

00:00:56.761 --> 00:00:58.238
The lobbying pipeline.

00:00:58.238 --> 00:01:05.227
Over forty-five registered lobbying communications targeted online harms legislation across multiple federal departments.

00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:12.284
OSINT analysis maps the institutional influence chain from lobbying registration through departmental contact to policy output.

00:01:12.284 --> 00:01:18.511
The same organizations that lobbied for content regulation now benefit from the regulatory framework they helped create.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.920
Charter Section 2(b) analysis.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:28.886
The pre-crime peace bond provision allows courts to impose restrictions before any offence occurs — inverting the fundamental presumption of innocence.

00:01:28.886 --> 00:01:34.318
Administrative hate speech complaints bypass criminal court standards established in the Whatcott test.

00:01:34.318 --> 00:01:38.522
The Digital Safety Commission makes content decisions outside the judicial system.

00:01:38.522 --> 00:01:44.363
These provisions create a regulatory architecture that can suppress lawful expression under the guise of safety.
